course: everyday
lesson: 126
title: Goodbye, please, thank you and sorry
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: A visit ends well with a goodbye: you find the host and say thank you, you walk a visitor to the door, and you go without a fuss when your parent says it is time.
explain: "Please" and "thank you" are for everyone, not only family: the café worker, the dinner lady, the bus driver and the parent who cooks dinner every day.
explain: A real apology takes responsibility: "Sorry I called you stupid" is real, "Sorry if you were upset" is not, and sorry means you try not to do it again.
explain: When someone says sorry to you, you say "That's okay" or "Thank you for saying sorry" and try to forgive, so the friendship can go on.
explain: A boy in Brisbane yells at his sister when angry; later he says "Sorry I shouted at you", and he really does stop shouting, so she believes him the next time.
fact: When you leave a party you find the host and the birthday child and say goodbye and thank you.
fact: When your parent says it is time to go, you finish quickly, say goodbye and go without a fuss.
fact: People say goodbye in many ways, like "See ya", "Cheers", "Take care" or "Catch you later".
fact: Saying goodbye or thank you to the bus driver or teacher thanks them for looking after you.
fact: Walking a visitor to the door shows they were welcome.
fact: You say "please" when you ask and "thank you" when you get something, also to staff in shops and cafés.
fact: Jobs done every day, like cooking dinner, take time and love and deserve thanks.
fact: A real apology says sorry for what you did, not only "sorry if you were upset".
fact: Sorry means you will try not to do the same thing again.
fact: When someone apologises, you say "That's okay" or "Thank you for saying sorry" and try to forgive.
fact: When you are late to class, you say "Sorry I'm late" and sit down quietly.
exercise: You are leaving a birthday party. What do you do? | Find the host and birthday child and say goodbye and thank you | Slip out quietly so you do not interrupt the host or the other children | Take an extra party bag and go | Say goodbye only to your best friend
exercise: Your parent says "Time to go" in the middle of your game. What is best? | Finish quickly, say goodbye and go without a fuss | Argue until you are allowed ten more minutes to finish the game | Hide so they cannot find you | Leave without saying goodbye to anyone
exercise: Which of these is an English goodbye? | "Catch you later" | "Hey, how's it going?" | "Good morning to you" | "Nice to meet you"
exercise: You get off the school bus at the end of the day. What is polite? | Say goodbye or thank you to the driver | Run off without looking at the driver | Wave only at your friends | Tell the driver to drive faster tomorrow
exercise: A visitor is leaving your home. What do you do? | Walk them to the door and say goodbye | Shout goodbye from the sofa | Keep watching TV until they have gone | Tell them to find their own way out
exercise: You order a hot chocolate in a café. What do you say? | "Please" when you ask and "Thank you" when you get it | Just "Hot chocolate" and nothing else | "Thank you" only if it is free, otherwise you are paying for it | Nothing, because the staff are paid
exercise: The cook in the lunch hall gives you your lunch. What do you say? | "Thank you" | Nothing, because it is their job | "Is that all?" | "Hurry up, please"
exercise: Your mum cooks dinner every day. What is a good thing to do? | Thank her for the meal sometimes | Never mention it, since it happens every day | Only thank her once a year on her birthday | Complain when it is not your favourite
exercise: You said something unkind when you were angry. Which is a real apology? | "Sorry I called you stupid" | "Sorry if you were upset by it" | "Sorry, but you started it first" | "Sorry, you are too sensitive"
exercise: You keep doing the same thing after saying sorry. What is wrong? | Sorry means you change the behaviour too | Nothing, because you already said sorry | You did not say sorry loudly enough | You should have said sorry twice
exercise: A friend apologises for breaking your pencil. What do you say? | "That's okay" or "Thank you for saying sorry" | "I will never forgive you" | Nothing, to show you are still cross about your favourite pencil | "You must buy me ten pencils"
exercise: You arrive late to class. What do you do? | Say "Sorry I'm late" and sit down quietly | Explain the whole story loudly to the class | Walk in and say nothing | Wait outside until the next lesson
test: A girl in Ohio says "Sorry if you got upset" after she laughed at her friend's drawing. Why is it not a real apology? | It does not take responsibility for what she did | It is too short to count | She should have written it down | Real apologies must be said in front of the class
test: Why do we thank a bus driver who is paid to drive? | People who look after us deserve thanks, paid or not | Because the driver might stop the bus and make you get off otherwise | Because thanking makes the bus go faster | Because it is a rule only on school buses
test: A boy says sorry to his brother for hitting him, then hits him again the next day. What has he not understood? | Sorry means trying not to do it again | Sorry must always be said twice | Sorry only counts in the morning | Sorry is only needed for big things, not for a bit of hitting
test: Your friend's dad drove you home after a sleepover. What should you do as you get out? | Say thank you and goodbye | Get out and run straight inside | Ask him to drive you to the park too | Wave at the car but say nothing
test: A girl in Auckland says "Cheers, see ya!" to her friend. What is she doing? | Saying goodbye in a friendly way | Asking for a drink | Saying hello to her for the first time that day | Saying sorry for being late
test: Someone says "I'm sorry I took your seat." Which answer helps the friendship go on? | "Thank you for saying sorry, that's okay" | "You always do that, go away" | "I will tell the teacher anyway" | Turning your back without answering so they know how upset you are
test: Why is leaving a party without a word a problem? | It can seem rude or ungrateful to the host | The host will not know how many cakes are left | Party bags are only given to those who say goodbye | Parties cannot end until everyone says goodbye
test: You come into a lesson ten minutes late because the bus was slow. What is the polite way in? | Say "Sorry I'm late" quietly and sit down | Burst in and tell everyone about the late bus | Stand at the door until the teacher notices | Go home since the lesson has already started
test: party's over, mums at the door, just leg it out? | Find the host and birthday child and say goodbye and thank you | Run out, they're busy anyway and won't even notice that you've gone | Wave from the car | Grab the party bag and go
test: said sorry if ur upset, that's an apology innit | No, a real apology says sorry for what you did | Yes, that's the best kind, because it shows you care about their feelings | Yes, any sorry counts | Only if you say it twice
test: he said sorry for taking my pen, what now | Say that's okay or thanks for saying sorry, and try to forgive | Ignore him for a week | Take his pen back to be even | Make him say it again in front of everyone so the whole class hears
test: late for class cos the bus, oh and my laces broke, what do i say | Say sorry I'm late and sit down quietly | Tell the whole story loudly so everyone knows it wasn't your fault | Say nothing and sneak in | Stand at the door until asked
explain: A boy in Leeds never said sorry after he knocked his friend's tower down on purpose; his friend stopped playing with him, and the fight lasted for weeks.
explain: A girl in Texas left a party without saying goodbye or thanks; the host's mum thought something was wrong and phoned around worried until she was found at home.
fact: A real sorry includes saying what you did and trying to put it right.
fact: Telling the adult in charge when you leave a place helps keep you safe, because they know where you are.
test: knocked over my mate's lego on purpose, sorry is cringe | Say sorry and help fix it, or he may stop playing with you | Never say sorry, it's weak | Knock down some of his other things too so that it's fair on both sides | Blame his brother
test: leaving the party, not telling anyone, just go | Say thanks and bye to the adult so they know you've left | Sneak out the back | Just send a text later | Leave your coat as a sign
test: said sorry but kept doing the same thing again | A real sorry means stopping and putting it right | Saying it is enough every time | Sorry fixes everything | Say it louder next time
test: omg did u hear Jess just walked out of Mia's party, didn't tell anyone, her mum rang the police n they searched the whole park!! | Tell the adult in charge when you leave; the police bit may be exaggerated | Leaving quietly is polite so nobody fusses | Police always come and search whenever a child leaves a party without telling anyone | Hide next time so nobody notices you leaving
test: Tom said sorry to Ben for the 50th time but keeps kicking him, everyone says Tom's a total psycho, spread it | Don't spread name-calling; a real sorry means he stops and puts it right | Spread it, everyone should know | Tell Ben he has to forgive the kicking | Saying sorry 50 times makes it all fine, even if the kicking still carries on
test: apparently the bus driver cried cos no one ever says thanks, my brother's mate saw it, is that real | Maybe or maybe not, but saying thanks to the driver is kind anyway | It must be true, a mate saw it | Bus drivers don't need any thanks | Only adults should thank the bus driver
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises a genuine apology? | It names what one did and seeks to put it right | It says "sorry if you were upset" and no more | It is said loudly so that others may hear it | It is repeated whenever the same act recurs
exam: Ascertain the appropriate conduct upon departure from a party. | Find the host and the birthday child and say thank you | Leave quietly so as not to disturb the guests | Send a message of thanks the following week | Take an extra party bag on the way out
exam: It is given that another person has apologised to you. Identify the recommended response. | "That's okay" or "Thank you for saying sorry", and forgive | Demand a second and longer apology at once | Ignore the apology until the next day | Reply that the matter can never be forgiven
exam: Identify the principal reason for informing the adult in charge when one leaves a place. | So that they know where one is and one stays safe | So that they may arrange a gift for one's family | So that they may check one has thanked everyone | So that they may close the door behind one
